mplex=True)\nk = sp.symbols('k', real=True)\n\n# Define the equations\ncircle_eq = sp.Abs(25 + 20*sp.I - z) - 5\nbisector_eq = sp.Abs(z - 4 - k) - sp.Abs(z - 3*sp.I - k)\n\n# Substitute the bisector equation into the circle equation\nz_expr = sp.symbols('x', real=True) + sp.I * sp.symbols('y', real=True)\ncircle_eq_sub = circle_eq.subs(z, 4 + k + 3*sp.I + z_expr)\nbisector_eq_sub = bisector_eq.subs(z, 4 + k + 3*sp.I + z_expr)\n\n# Solve for k\nk_values = sp.solve(circle_eq_sub, k)\nk_values = [sp.simplify(k_val) for k_val in k_values]\n\n# Sum of all possible values of k\nsum_k = sum(k_values)\nsum_k_simplified = sp.simplify(sum_k)\nsum_k_mn = (sum_k_simplified.as_numer_denom())\n\nprint(sum_k_mn)\n```\n```output\nNo algorithms are implemented to solve equation Abs(25 + 20*I - (4 + k + 3*I + (x + I*y)))/5 - Abs(4 + k + 3*I + (x + I*y) - 3*I - k)\n```\nIt seems that sympy is having difficulty solving the equation directly due to the complex nature of the expressions. Instead, we can take a more geometric approach to find the values of $k$.\n\nGiven the system of equations:\n1. $|25+20i-z|=5$\n2. $|z-4-k|=|z-3i-k|$\n\nWe know that the second equation represents a perpendicular bisector of the segment joining $4+k$ and $3i+k$. The center of this perpendicular bisector is the midpoint of this segment, which is $\\left(2+\\frac{k}{2}+\\frac{3i}{2}\\right)$. The slope of the segment is 1, so the perpendicular bisector will have a slope of $-1$.\n\nThe equation of the perpendicular bisector can be written as:\n\\[ y - \\frac{3i}{2} = -1 \\left( x - \\left(2+\\frac{k}{2}\\right) \\right) \\]\n\\[ y = -x + 2 + \\frac{k}{2} + \\frac{3i}{2} \\]\n\nThis line must intersect the circle $|25+20i-z|=5$ at exactly one point. The circle is centered at $25+20i$ with a radius of 5. The intersection of the line and the circle will give us the values of $z$.\n\nTo find the values of $k$ such that the line intersects the circle at exactly one point, we can substitute the equation of the line into the equation of the circle and solve for $k$. The equation of the circle can be written in terms of $x$ and $y$ as:\n\\[ |25+20i - (x + iy)| = 5 \\]\n\\[ |25 - x + (20 - y)i| = 5 \\]\n\\[ \\sqrt{(25 - x)^2 + (20 - y)^2} = 5 \\]\n\\[ (25 - x)^2 + (20 - y)^2 = 25 \\]\n\nSubstituting the equation of the line $y = -x + 2 + \\frac{k}{2} + \\frac{3i}{2}$ into the circle equation, we get:\n\\[ (25 - x)^2 + \\left(20 - \\left(-x + 2 + \\frac{k}{2} + \\frac{3i}{2}\\right)\\right)^2 = 25 \\]\n\\[ (25 - x)^2 + \\left(20 + x - 2 - \\frac{k}{2} - \\frac{3i}{2}\\right)^2 = 25 \\]\n\\[ (25 - x)^2 + \\left(18 + x - \\frac{k}{2} - \\frac{3i}{2}\\right)^2 = 25 \\]\n\nThis equation is quite complex to solve directly. Instead, we can use the geometric properties of the circle and the perpendicular bisector to find the values of $k$.\n\nThe perpendicular bisector will intersect the circle at exactly one point if the distance from the center of the circle to the line is equal to the radius of the circle. The distance from the center of the circle $(25, 20)$ to the line $y = -x + 2 + \\frac{k}{2} + \\frac{3i}{2}$ can be calculated using the point-to-line distance formula. The distance from a point $(x_1, y_1)$ to a line $Ax + By + C = 0$ is given by:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\frac{|Ax_1 + By_1 + C|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}} \\]\n\nFor our line $y = -x + 2 + \\frac{k}{2} + \\frac{3i}{2}$, we can rewrite it as $x + y - 2 - \\frac{k}{2} - \\frac{3i}{2} = 0$. The distance from the center $(25, 20)$ to this line is:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\frac{|25 + 20 - 2 - \\frac{k}{2} - \\frac{3i}{2}|}{\\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2}} = \\frac{|23 - \\frac{k}{2} - \\frac{3i}{2}|}{\\sqrt{2}} \\]\n\nThis distance must be equal to the radius of the circle, which is 5. So we have:\n\\[ \\frac{|23 - \\frac{k}{2} - \\frac{3i}{2}|}{\\sqrt{2}} = 5 \\]\n\\[ |23 - \\frac{k}{2} - \\frac{3i}{2}| = 5\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\\[ \\sqrt{\\left(23 - \\frac{k}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2} = 5\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\\[ \\left(23 - \\frac{k}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2 = (5\\sqrt{2})^2 \\]\n\\[ \\left(23 - \\frac{k}{2}\\right)^2 + \\frac{9}{4} = 50 \\]\n\\[ \\left(23 - \\frac{k}{2}\\right)^2 = 41.75 \\]\n\\[ 23 - \\frac{k}{2} = \\pm \\sqrt{41.75} \\]\n\\[ 23 - \\frac{k}{2} = \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{167}}{2} \\]\n\\[ 23 - \\frac{k}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{167}}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 23 - \\frac{k}{2} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{167}}{2} \\]\n\\[ 23 - \\frac{k}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{167}}{2} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k = 46 - \\sqrt{167} \\]\n\\[ 23 - \\frac{k}{2} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{167}}{2} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k = 46 + \\sqrt{167} \\]\n\nThe sum of these values of $k$ is:\n\\[ (46 - \\sqrt{167}) + (46 + \\sqrt{167}) = 92 \\]\n\nSo the sum of all possible values of $k$ is $\\frac{92}{1}$, and the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{92} \\]",
